WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.673
Industrial network telemetry describes communication among HMIs, PLCs, RTUs, SCADA servers,

00:00:04.673 --> 00:00:07.583
historians, engineering workstations, gateways, and remote-access systems.

00:00:07.583 --> 00:00:12.572
It can show source and destination, protocol, direction, timing, volume, session behavior,

00:00:12.572 --> 00:00:15.898
and in some cases the industrial operation represented.

00:00:15.898 --> 00:00:18.808
Stable OT networks often produce recognizable patterns.

00:00:18.808 --> 00:00:22.550
An HMI polls a controller, a historian collects values,

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.876
and an engineering workstation communicates during approved maintenance.

00:00:25.876 --> 00:00:28.371
New peers, changed frequency, unexpected protocols,

00:00:28.371 --> 00:00:32.944
or traffic crossing an unintended boundary can become useful investigation signals.

00:00:32.944 --> 00:00:36.685
Passive collection is commonly used to reduce disruption risk.

00:00:36.685 --> 00:00:41.674
Network taps or switch mirror ports copy existing traffic to a sensor

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.753
without asking devices to respond.

00:00:43.753 --> 00:00:47.910
Placement matters: sensors near industrial DMZs, control-area boundaries, remote links,

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:52.068
and key supervisory paths see different parts of the system.

00:00:52.068 --> 00:00:56.641
Collection design should account for redundancy, bandwidth, management access, and failure.

00:00:56.641 --> 00:01:03.293
A monitoring sensor should not become part of the control path or create an uncontrolled bridge.

00:01:03.293 --> 00:01:07.866
Captured data also reveals sensitive architecture and should be protected accordingly.

00:01:07.866 --> 00:01:09.945
Network telemetry has blind spots.

00:01:09.945 --> 00:01:14.518
It may miss serial segments, encrypted content, dormant devices, local actions,

00:01:14.518 --> 00:01:19.091
traffic inside an unobserved switch, or devices hidden behind a gateway.

00:01:19.091 --> 00:01:22.833
Metadata can still be valuable when content is unavailable,

00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:25.743
but confidence must reflect the actual coverage.

00:01:25.743 --> 00:01:28.653
Interpret communication with asset and process context.

00:01:28.653 --> 00:01:31.563
A protocol write is not automatically malicious,

00:01:31.563 --> 00:01:35.305
and a missing connection may reflect a planned shutdown.

00:01:35.305 --> 00:01:40.710
Network evidence becomes actionable when it is combined with maintenance records, device roles,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.620
endpoint logs, process trends, and engineering knowledge.
